---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PlaceholderImage from '../components/PlaceholderImage.astro';
import PullQuote from '../components/PullQuote.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="How a corporate career turned into a ceramics practice, and why that led to wanting to teach."
  active="about"
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 pb-4 pt-14 sm:px-8 sm:pt-20">
    <h1 class="font-display text-4xl text-ink sm:text-5xl">About</h1>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-10 sm:px-8">
    <div class="grid gap-10 sm:grid-cols-5 sm:gap-14">
      <div class="sm:col-span-3">
        <p class="max-w-measure leading-relaxed text-clay">
          For a few years, my life looked like most people's do after college: a job with a
          title, a commute, a calendar full of meetings. It was fine. It just wasn't mine in any
          particular way. I don't think I realized how much I was missing a place to make
          something with my hands until I found one.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5 max-w-measure leading-relaxed text-clay">
          I signed up for a beginner wheel-throwing class on a whim, mostly because a friend
          didn't want to go alone. I was bad at it &mdash; genuinely, comically bad. Clay went
          everywhere. Nothing I made in that first class was usable. But something about sitting
          at the wheel, focused entirely on one small, physical problem, felt like a kind of
          quiet I hadn't had in a long time.
        </p>
      </div>
      <PlaceholderImage
        alt="Early photograph from the artist's first ceramics classes"
        ratio="aspect-[3/4]"
        class="sm:col-span-2"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-10 sm:px-8">
    <div class="grid gap-10 sm:grid-cols-5 sm:gap-14">
      <PlaceholderImage
        alt="Photograph of shelves of finished work from a community studio"
        ratio="aspect-[3/4] sm:order-1"
        class="sm:order-1 sm:col-span-2"
      />
      <div class="sm:order-2 sm:col-span-3">
        <p class="max-w-measure leading-relaxed text-clay">
          What started as one class became a weekly habit, and then something closer to a
          second life. Over the next several years, I moved around for work &mdash; San
          Francisco, then Boston, then New York &mdash; and each time, one of the first things I
          did in a new city was find a community studio. Looking back, that says a lot about how
          much ceramics had come to matter to me.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5 max-w-measure leading-relaxed text-clay">
          Each studio had its own personality. San Francisco taught me technique &mdash; patient,
          exacting instructors who cared about the fundamentals. Boston is where I first started
          glazing with any real intention, and where I made my first pieces I was genuinely proud
          of. New York is where I started thinking about ceramics as something I might teach
          someday, not just make.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Featured pull quote — the one place on the site where the copy is
       allowed to take up real visual space. -->
  <section class="border-y border-mist/40 bg-mist-50">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 py-16 sm:px-8 sm:py-24">
      <PullQuote>
        Ceramics surprised me. I didn't grow up making art, and I didn't expect to find a
        creative practice that would become such an important part of my life. What started as
        trying something completely new gradually became the place where I learned patience,
        embraced imperfection, and discovered a part of myself I hadn't explored before. That's
        one of the reasons I care so deeply about teaching. You never know which student is
        trying ceramics on a whim and is about to discover something that changes how they see
        themselves.
      </PullQuote>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-14 sm:px-8 sm:py-20">
    <div class="grid gap-10 sm:grid-cols-5 sm:gap-14 sm:items-center">
      <div class="sm:col-span-3">
        <p class="max-w-measure leading-relaxed text-clay">
          These days, my practice looks different than it did five years ago. I throw larger,
          more deliberate forms. I've stopped being precious about pieces that don't survive the
          kiln &mdash; some of my favorite work has come out of glaze tests I expected to fail.
          Mostly, I've gotten more comfortable with the parts of the process I can't fully
          control, which is maybe the most useful thing ceramics has taught me.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5 max-w-measure leading-relaxed text-clay">
          What hasn't changed is why I keep coming back: the studios that shaped me were never
          just about technique. They were places where people showed up for each other every
          week. I'd like to help build that for someone else &mdash; which is why I'm ready to
          teach.
        </p>
      </div>
      <PlaceholderImage
        alt="Recent photograph of the artist's current studio space and tools"
        ratio="aspect-[4/5]"
        class="sm:col-span-2"
      />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
